v2024-5077.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n200400665740uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo233288704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://pubs.usgs.gov/sir/2024/5077/sir20245077.pdf  aC0bGPO  abc109 20260806b20260806  aDOCDISCOVERYb20260806  aVEND01b2026082603895cam a2200493 i 00147756920260806163426.0m     o  d s      cr |||||||||||260423s2026    dcu     ob   s000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)1586934463  aMLRbengerdacMLRdOCLCOdGPO  apcc  an-us-mn  aXZL4  a1016-C (online)0 aY 4.G 74/7:C 82/1604aThe Cost of Doing Nothing :bHow Tim Walz and Keith Ellison Fueled Minnesota's Fraud Explosion. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bHouse Committee on Oversight and Government Reform,c2026.  a1 online resource (53 pages)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"March 4, 2026."  a"An Interim Staff Report by the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform Majority Staff."  a"In December 2025, the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform launched an investigation into fraud in Minnesota's social services programs that occurred under Governor Tim Walz and Attorney General Keith Ellison's watch. The Committee's investigation has found that senior officials in Minnesota state government, including Governor Walz and Attorney General Ellison, were aware of widespread fraud in federally funded social services programs for years, possessed the legal and procedural authority to stop payments, but repeatedly failed to act. As a result, potentially billions of American taxpayer dollars were allowed to flow to fraudulent actors, while vulnerable populations were harmed and whistleblowers were ignored, sidelined, and retaliated against. While the Committee's investigation remains ongoing, testimony and documents obtained to date establish a consistent pattern: fraud warnings were elevated to senior levels of the Minnesota state government, meaningful corrective action was delayed or avoided, and payments continued long after credible red flags emerged. The Committee's findings raise serious questions about governance and oversight in Minnesota's administration of federal funds. The failure to act decisively in the face of known fraud allowed criminal schemes to flourish and diverted resources away from eligible recipients: the vulnerable populations these programs were intended to serve."--Page 2.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (viewed April 23, 2026).10aWalz, Tim,d1964-0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n202405350010aEllison, Keith,d1963-0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n201300178010aMinnesota.bGovernor (2019- : Walz)10aMinnesota.bAttorney General.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n81018158 0aLegislative oversightzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85075838 0aFraudzMinnesota. 0aExecutive departmentszMinnesota. 0aAdministrative agencieszMinnesota.1 aUnited States.bCongress.bHouse.bCommittee on Oversight and Government Reform,eauthor,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200703483940uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo258817704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://oversight.house.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/The-Cost-of-Doing-Nothing_How-Tim-Walz-and-Keith-Ellison-Fueled-Minnesotas-Fraud-Explosion_3.4.26_FINAL.pdf  aC0bGPO  abc34 20260806b20260806922  aBIBCONEWb20260806922  aVEND01b2026082603289cam a2200529 i 00147758620260807095154.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||260807s2026    dcu     ob   f000 0 eng c8 a64-224q(GPO jacket number)  a(OCoLC)1610531496  aGPObengerdaepncGPOdGPO  apcc  an-us---  aXZL4  a1016-C (online)0 aY 4.OV 2:119-69  aSerial no. 